1) IMS Core (based on Kamailio as open-source SIP (Session Integration Protocol) engine/server/proxy)
Includes IMS functions:
P-CSCF / I-CSCF / S-CSCF
SIP routing, registration, call control
Reality:
Companies like Kontron:
Customize it
Integrate it
Add telecom features (security, scaling, HA)
👉 They usually don’t build IMS from scratch, but:
✔ adapt
✔ extend
✔ productize it

2) MCX(Misssion Critical Systems) / MCPTT-MCData-MCVideo (like Walkie-talkie over LTE runs on top of IMS)
This is their core product.
They build:
MCPTT server (push-to-talk logic)
Group communication
Floor control
Priority / emergency handling
Dispatcher integration
MCData / MCVideo services
👉 This is 100% custom telecom software
✔ This is where most engineering effort goes
✔ This is what differentiates vendors

3) RTP(Real-time Transport Protocol) / Media handling
Includes:
RTP engine / proxy
Media routing, duplication, recording
Reality:
Often:
Open-source (RTPengine)
Or third-party vendor
Companies like Kontron:
Integrate it
Configure it
Connect it with MCPTT logic
👉 Usually not fully developed by them

4) HSS (Home Subscriber Server) / Subscriber database = Phonebook
Includes:
User profiles
Authentication
Permissions
Reality:
Sometimes:
Third-party
OR built-in lightweight version
👉 Kontron may:
✔ integrate
✔ sometimes provide their own simplified version

5) Signaling → handled by "SIP" Session Initiation Protocol
   Media(audio/video) → handled by "RTP" Real-time Transport Protocol
   Authentication/Authorization → handled by "HSS" Home Subscriber Server
   Service control/orchestration → handled by "IMS" IP Multimedia Subsystem
SIP = “set up the call”
RTP = “carry the voice”
HSS = “who are you?”
IMS = “run the service”
MCX = “special mission-critical features”
LTE/5G = “the road everything travels on”
